#lang turmeric refined
;; RT0 graduation compatibility promise (2026-08-01): `refined` graduated, so
;; its LANG_LAYERS[] row is deleted -- CLAUDE.md is explicit that layers
;; graduate to always-on rather than accumulating.  But a deleted row makes
;; every file that still names the token fail outright with TUR-E0330, which is
;; a harsher landing than the same graduation gives the CLI flag.
;;
;; `GRADUATED_LAYERS[]` in src/compiler/lang_layers.c is the layer-side shim
;; that closes that gap: the token is accepted and ignored with a one-time
;; TUR-W0064, so a file carrying `#lang turmeric refined` keeps compiling
;; across the boundary.  The shim landed EMPTY ahead of this graduation on
;; purpose -- adding it afterwards would have shipped one release in which
;; every such file broke.
;;
;; This fixture was `refine-lang-layer`, which pinned the layer turning
;; discharge ON.  There is nothing to turn on any more; what is worth pinning
;; is that the token is still accepted.  Discharge itself is unconditional, so
;; `(shift-up 3)` is still proved.
(defn shift-up [x : #refine{ v : int | (>= v 3) }] : #refine{ r : int | (> r 0) }
  (- x 2))

(defn main [] : int
  (println (shift-up 3))
  (println "refine-graduated-lang-layer-noop")
  0)
